98
00:15:32,560 --> 00:15:36,240
Now, let's continue our journey through urban China.

99
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:53,600
By some accounts the largest municipality in China, Chongqing is 
a mindboggling place, and our next destination.

100
00:15:59,360 --> 00:16:06,840
Located on the mighty Yangtze river, it has become an 
important commercial and transportation hub,

101
00:16:06,840 --> 00:16:12,320
attracting people from around the region and beyond to work and live.

102
00:16:57,600 --> 00:17:02,000
A traveler's first impression of Chongqing can be overwhelming,

103
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:08,720
as the city is built on steep mountains and hills,
making it somewhat challenging to navigate.

104
00:17:09,760 --> 00:17:20,240
Gladly, a cable car spanning the Yangtze, and a light-railway line,
meandering through the tall skyscrapers, make it easier to get around.

105
00:17:23,440 --> 00:17:29,160
Nevertheless, visitors should be prepared to climb a lot of stairs. 

106
00:17:35,680 --> 00:17:46,480
Chongqing lacks the attractions that other cities have, but it's a fascinating place
to witness some of the urbanization process that takes place across the country.

107
00:17:48,560 --> 00:17:54,320
While China used to be a country of farmers, it is quickly
becoming a nation of city dwellers,

108
00:17:54,320 --> 00:17:59,920
and in Chongqing you'll stumble on a constrasting image 
at almost every corner. 

109
00:18:04,520 --> 00:18:10,800
Old neighborhoods need to make way for shopping malls,
offices, and residential apartment towers,

110
00:18:11,040 --> 00:18:15,840
destroying one way of life, trying to create another.

111
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:37,160
We continue our journey to Lhasa, the spiritual heartland of Tibet,
located on the 'Rooftop of the World'.

112
00:18:41,280 --> 00:18:46,680
While there are other ways to reach Lhasa, I found the most
interesting one by train,

113
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:54,600
traveling on the highest railway line in the world, over 5,000 meters at some stretches.

114
00:19:14,400 --> 00:19:26,280
Traversing the rugged, barren Tibetan landscapes proved a beautiful introduction
to arriving in Lhasa, which felt like stepping into a different world.

115
00:19:28,080 --> 00:19:33,680
The first views of the magnificent Potala Palace are unforgettable. 

116
00:19:35,480 --> 00:19:43,000
This is the historical seat of the Tibetan government,
and former Winter residence of the Dalai Lama,

117
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:50,560
the spiritual leader of the Tibetan people, who is currently
living in exile in India.

118
00:19:54,400 --> 00:19:59,880
Tibet has had a tumultuous history, dating back to the 7th century,

119
00:19:59,880 --> 00:20:07,120
and has known periods of independence and occupation,
by Chinese and Mongolian dynasties. 

120
00:20:10,080 --> 00:20:18,880
More recently, in 1950, it was invaded by Communist China,
which has ruled the region ever since.

121
00:20:20,640 --> 00:20:27,760
Much has been written about the Tibetan issue, and it's a good idea
to read up before you travel here.

122
00:20:28,040 --> 00:20:34,640
And note that foreign travelers can only visit Tibet as part of a group tour.

123
00:20:49,600 --> 00:20:56,200
A good way to start exploring Lhasa is by joining pilgrims 
making a traditional 'kora',

124
00:20:56,200 --> 00:21:02,000
as they walk around the Potala Palace turning prayer wheels
and mumbling mantras.

125
00:21:04,360 --> 00:21:10,920
Kora is a combination of pilgrimage and meditative practices in Tibetan Buddhism.

126
00:21:16,880 --> 00:21:22,400
It's also possible to climb the Potala Palace, but make sure to take your time,

127
00:21:22,400 --> 00:21:28,400
as Lhasa is located at an altitude of over 3,600 meters.

128
00:21:36,440 --> 00:21:46,280
Another spot not to miss is the Sera Monastery, one of the largest in Tibet,
and an important center of education.

129
00:21:50,320 --> 00:22:00,000
Come here for the daily debating session, when monks discuss
philosophical and religious topics inside of their courtyards.

130
00:22:08,120 --> 00:22:15,120
My favorite area in Lhasa is called Barkhor, essentially the 
spiritual center of the city.

131
00:22:16,040 --> 00:22:22,080
Countless pilgrims walk around the Jokhang temple,
where they perform prostration,

132
00:22:22,520 --> 00:22:27,800
a ritual meant to purify negative thoughts and bad karma.

133
00:22:29,160 --> 00:22:39,520
Some prostrate all the way from their villages to Lhasa, a journey that can
take several months, but most take part in shorter versions.

134
00:23:12,320 --> 00:23:22,000
After Lhasa, we travel to Central China, to visit Xian,
one of the four ancient Chinese capitals,

135
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:28,160
located in a region that was at the heart of early Chinese civilization.

136
00:23:31,400 --> 00:23:38,320
Known as Chang'an in the older days, it was at the center of several dynasties,

137
00:23:38,320 --> 00:23:43,920
and flourished as the beginning and end of the historic Silk Road.

138
00:23:47,440 --> 00:23:58,800
Before its demise in the 10th century, this was a place where Chinese culture
and creativity thrived, and many religions coexisted.

139
00:24:02,440 --> 00:24:10,680
While Xian today is mostly a modern city, reminders of its history
are scattered in and around town.

140
00:24:13,760 --> 00:24:22,120
The City Walls, although constructed in the 14th century, 
are a good example of how imperial cities were built.

141
00:24:23,880 --> 00:24:33,240
It's almost hard to imagine that the walls of ancient Chang'an
originally enclosed an area seven times larger than this.

142
00:24:43,720 --> 00:24:53,720
One of my favorite spots in Xian is the Muslim neighborhood,
home to the city's Hui community for centuries.

143
00:25:06,840 --> 00:25:14,360
Although the area has undergone many changes, and parts are now
mostly geared towards tourism,

144
00:25:14,360 --> 00:25:21,280
it's still a good place to wander around, meet the people,
and try some of the delicious food. 

145
00:25:54,200 --> 00:25:59,040
Xian's most famous place, however, lies outside the city.

146
00:25:59,680 --> 00:26:06,640
The magnificent Terracotta Army is located around 40 kilometers of downtown Xian,

147
00:26:06,640 --> 00:26:10,680
and is one of China's most impressive historic sites.

148
00:26:14,360 --> 00:26:27,960
The Army, consisting of thousands of life size warriors, chariots, and others,
depicts the armies of Qin Shi Huang, the first Emperor of a unified China.

149
00:26:32,480 --> 00:26:39,080
It was built underground, around the tomb of the Emperor,
more than two millennia ago,

150
00:26:39,080 --> 00:26:44,880
and was only discovered in 1974 by local farmers.

151
00:26:54,720 --> 00:27:01,720
We continue our travels to Ordos, a somewhat surreal city in the North of China.

152
00:27:03,400 --> 00:27:10,000
The reason for coming here was to see one of the country's best known 'ghost towns'.

153
00:27:12,360 --> 00:27:20,840
In the Chinese context, these are cities or neighborhoods that are
newly constructed, but where very few people actually live.

154
00:27:46,240 --> 00:27:52,560
The reason these ghost towns exist is simply put for the sake of economic growth.

155
00:27:55,200 --> 00:28:00,600
Many Chinese analysts would say they are investments 
into future urbanization,

156
00:28:00,600 --> 00:28:09,080
while global experts argue they are an example of a growing
property bubble and housing crisis in the country.

157
00:28:17,280 --> 00:28:28,320
Walking the largely deserted streets, past lavish, but empty apertment buildings,
and through quiet shopping malls, did feel somewhat eerie.

158
00:28:43,760 --> 00:28:48,920
And as I traveled to other parts of China, such as here in Shenfu,

159
00:28:49,360 --> 00:28:55,240
I couldn't help but wonder what these places would look like in a few decades.

160
00:29:08,640 --> 00:29:15,880
Our next destination lies in far Western China, in the now 
controversial Xinjiang province.

161
00:29:19,120 --> 00:29:23,600
Kashgar is, or was, a city like no other.

162
00:29:25,480 --> 00:29:31,320
Surrounded by mountains and deserts, it is closer to Baghdad than Beijing,

163
00:29:31,320 --> 00:29:38,400
and its original inhabitants, the Uyghurs, are closely linked
to the people of Central Asia.

164
00:29:41,760 --> 00:29:51,320
It was an important stop on the ancient Silk Road, and has been at the
crossroads of cultures and religions for over two millennia.

165
00:29:56,240 --> 00:30:03,120
Please note that I visited Kashgar in 2010, and many things have changed since then.

166
00:30:24,680 --> 00:30:31,320
Before being demolished and rebuilt, Kashgar's old town was
where most travelers went,

167
00:30:31,320 --> 00:30:36,400
to get a sense of the traditions and customs of the Uyghur people.

168
00:30:39,400 --> 00:30:48,880
This was where craftsmen had been working for generations, creating
beautiful silverware, clothes, handicrafts, and other things.

169
00:30:50,240 --> 00:30:56,240
The narrow alleyways in these remaining pockets of old town
were filled with activities and charm,

170
00:30:56,760 --> 00:31:02,320
and I could spend hours, if not days, here just soaking up the atmosphere.

171
00:31:19,760 --> 00:31:29,040
And every Sunday, traders from around Kashgar gathered at a massive
livestock market, unrivaled in the region.

172
00:31:33,160 --> 00:31:41,480
Thousands of goats, cows, horses, donkeys, and other animals
invaded the town at sunrise,

173
00:31:41,480 --> 00:31:46,080
accompanied by farmers, nomads and shepherds.

174
00:31:53,800 --> 00:32:00,800
It was a fascinating sight, that could easily be the set of a movie.

175
00:32:51,160 --> 00:32:56,200
These traditional scenes, however, were disappearing quickly,

176
00:32:56,640 --> 00:33:03,960
after the Chinese government made Kashgar, and the entire province of 
Xinjiang, a so called 'top priority'. 

177
00:33:05,200 --> 00:33:13,760
In recent years, things escalated further, with around a million,
mostly Uyghur people, being detained,

178
00:33:13,760 --> 00:33:18,200
in what the Chinese government calls 're-education camps'.

179
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:24,200
This process has been condemned globally,

180
00:33:24,200 --> 00:33:31,240
and the treatment of the Uyghurs, in and out of these camps,
has been labeled a crime against humanity,

181
00:33:31,240 --> 00:33:36,120
by the UN and other international organizations.

182
00:33:45,960 --> 00:33:53,040
We can only hope that the situation in this region will somehow improve in the future.

183
00:34:07,280 --> 00:34:17,400
After Kashgar, we travel back to Eastern China, to visit Qingdao,
one of China's most beautiful coastal cities.

184
00:34:18,800 --> 00:34:26,840
Set on the shores of the Yellow Sea, with rolling green hills as 
backdrop, it surely is a scenic place.

185
00:34:47,840 --> 00:34:54,600
Many Chinese tourists come here to visit the beaches,
and eat some of the best seafood in the country.

186
00:34:54,600 --> 00:35:00,960
But it was the German architecture that caught my eye when I first arrived here.

187
00:35:03,480 --> 00:35:09,200
German naval officers invaded Qingdao in the late 19th century,

188
00:35:09,200 --> 00:35:17,520
and left their mark on the city, building railway lines, churches,
villas, schools, and even a brewery.

189
00:35:17,680 --> 00:35:22,760
Tsingtao is now one of China's largest beer producers.

190
00:35:26,640 --> 00:35:31,640
Perhaps the most striking building is Saint Michael's Catholic church.

191
00:35:31,840 --> 00:35:39,240
The church has a beautiful interior but equally interesting is
what often takes place on the outside. 

192
00:35:43,440 --> 00:35:51,160
Soon to be married brides and grooms, wearing Western style
dresses, take orders from busy camera crews.

193
00:35:53,160 --> 00:36:03,280
The wedding industry is booming in China, with a rising middle class able to
afford more extravagant festivities, including pre wedding photo shoots.

194
00:36:23,600 --> 00:36:33,920
Like many cities in China, Qingdao is also changing rapidly,
and the constrasts become bigger as you move into the suburbs.

195
00:36:51,360 --> 00:37:01,680
Here you can see everything from disappearing neighborhoods
and second hand markets, to wholesale companies and industrial centers.

196
00:37:44,080 --> 00:37:48,640
Qingdao's modern city center feels entirely different.

197
00:37:49,080 --> 00:37:54,080
It's only a short taxi or subway ride away from the old town,

198
00:37:56,120 --> 00:38:03,320
but I suggest using the waterfront promenade where you can 
walk through some beautiful areas of the city.

199
00:38:45,240 --> 00:38:49,880
We follow the coastline all the way South, to visit Hong Kong, 

200
00:38:49,880 --> 00:38:57,000
seen by many as the perfect fusion between East and West,
and one of my favorite cities in the world.

201
00:38:57,920 --> 00:39:03,000
It's a modern metropolis, but also a cultural powerhouse.

202
00:39:04,360 --> 00:39:13,320
It's easy to fall in love with Hong Kong at first sight, 
with its amazing skyline and backdrop of green mountains,

203
00:39:13,600 --> 00:39:18,320
but for many this is only where the story begins.

204
00:39:20,800 --> 00:39:24,800
The city was part of Britain for almost a century,

205
00:39:25,880 --> 00:39:32,640
the result of negotiations with the Chinese empire,
after the infamous Opium Wars.

206
00:39:34,320 --> 00:39:45,200
Over the years, with ups and downs, the people of Hong Kong managed
to build a flourishing society with democratic freedoms and their own identity.

207
00:39:47,560 --> 00:39:51,200
Some of these freedoms, however, have come under pressure,

208
00:39:51,200 --> 00:39:56,240
after the city was handed back to China in 1997.

209
00:39:58,720 --> 00:40:07,520
Recently, the city has seen massive protest, with people
voicing their concern over China's growing influence.

210
00:40:25,880 --> 00:40:32,920
One of the best ways to start exploring Hong Kong, is to 
visit the spectacular Victoria Peak,

211
00:40:32,920 --> 00:40:38,520
from where you can admire the jaw-dropping urban landscape of the city.

212
00:40:42,040 --> 00:40:46,240
You can reach the Peak by taxi or on a steep hike, 

213
00:40:46,240 --> 00:40:54,760
but most travelers use the historic Victoria Peak Tram,
a gravity defying attraction that should not be missed.

214
00:41:00,240 --> 00:41:07,840
Another way to appreciate the city's skyline is to sail the 
classic Star Ferry, across Victoria Harbour.

215
00:41:07,840 --> 00:41:14,120
For just a few Hong Kong dollars, this is probably
the best valued trip in the city.

216
00:41:24,360 --> 00:41:33,960
Hong Kong is a breeze to get around, and boasts a very efficient
public transport system, including an extensive metro network,

217
00:41:33,960 --> 00:41:39,680
double deck trams, and even helicopters for those who can afford it.

218
00:41:44,840 --> 00:41:49,520
For some travelers, Hong Kong is a shopper's paradise.

219
00:41:49,520 --> 00:41:59,080
And sometimes it feels like the city is one giant shopping mall,
as many buildings are connected by airconditioned walkways.

220
00:42:05,480 --> 00:42:10,880
Others come here for the Cantonese food, which is some of the best in Asia.

221
00:42:10,880 --> 00:42:18,640
From luxurious restaurants to street food markets and local eateries,
there is something for everyone.

222
00:42:27,760 --> 00:42:33,400
For a different side of Hong Kong, head to some of the tranquil islands,

223
00:42:33,400 --> 00:42:41,280
or visit the Big Buddha statue, located in a major Buddhist 
center in the mountains.

224
00:42:49,280 --> 00:42:56,400
But it's the streets of Kowloon district and beyond,
that really make me love Hong Kong.

225
00:42:57,360 --> 00:43:05,160
This is the cultural heartland of the city, where traditional
neighborhoods mix with bustling street markets.

226
00:43:06,040 --> 00:43:11,960
It feels overwhelming in some parts, yet surprisingly peaceful in others.

227
00:43:23,480 --> 00:43:29,880
Visit one of the many temples, browse some of the markets,
sample local cuisine,

228
00:43:29,880 --> 00:43:35,160
or simply wander around and be surprised by this magnificent city.

229
00:44:06,800 --> 00:44:17,320
A stone's throw away from Hong Kong lies Macau, a former
Portuguese enclave that is known as the 'Las Vegas of the East'.

230
00:44:18,840 --> 00:44:24,280
Its skyline is dominated by some of the world's largest casinos,

231
00:44:24,280 --> 00:44:29,400
and parts of the city have turned into massive entertainment centers. 

232
00:44:46,720 --> 00:44:54,080
The reason for its popularity is simple, it's the only place 
in China where gambling is legal.

233
00:45:38,960 --> 00:45:51,040
But Macau also has a different face, with a Portuguese influence
that is still visible today, in its architecture, food, and culture.

234
00:45:52,560 --> 00:46:01,360
Even though you'll share the streets with a lot of tourists,
it's a fun and interesting place to spend a day or two.

235
00:46:26,520 --> 00:46:36,680
Bordering Hong Kong on the North, Shenzhen is one of China's
most remarkable cities, and our next destination.

236
00:46:41,280 --> 00:46:50,280
A small fishing village merely 40 years ago, Shenzhen has grown 
into one of the country's largest and wealthiest cities.

237
00:46:53,440 --> 00:47:00,160
It attracts businesspeople, migrant workers, and investors
from around the country and the world,

238
00:47:00,320 --> 00:47:03,640
all chasing the 'Chinese economic dream'.

239
00:47:31,760 --> 00:47:40,560
Shenzhen became a so called 'Special Economic Zone',
an idea brought forward by Deng Xiaoping,

240
00:47:40,560 --> 00:47:46,400
who led a series of market economy reforms,
that propelled the growth of China.

241
00:47:49,680 --> 00:47:57,600
Since then, Shenzhen has become China's largest electronics manufacturing
hub, and is now a leading high-tech center.

242
00:48:47,000 --> 00:48:51,120
For travelers, Shenzhen may not offer a lot of sights.

243
00:48:51,120 --> 00:48:58,520
But there are plenty of parks, shopping centers, and a surprising
theme park that can keep you busy.

244
00:49:27,880 --> 00:49:39,320
In general, I found Shenzhen a young and dynamic place where you can get a sense
of China's urbanization and economic expansion of the past decades.

245
00:50:53,240 --> 00:51:00,160
While you can get around China in numerous ways, the high-speed 
railway network is by far the most convenient,

246
00:51:00,160 --> 00:51:03,520
especially when you travel between larger cities.

247
00:51:05,600 --> 00:51:16,120
Because of their size, the train stations can sometimes feel intimidating
to navigate, but once you understand the system, it's all quite straightforward.

248
00:52:03,760 --> 00:52:08,360
We travel back to Central China, to visit Wuhan,

249
00:52:08,360 --> 00:52:14,840
a city that will forever be linked to the global pandemic, 
that changed our world so abruptly. 

250
00:52:28,560 --> 00:52:34,320
I visited Wuhan twice before this all started, and met a sprawling city,

251
00:52:34,480 --> 00:52:44,080
with massive bridges spanning the Yangtze river, busy shopping streets,
but also some charismatic old neighborhoods.

252
00:53:36,880 --> 00:53:46,320
Wuhan is actually a combination of three formerly independent cities,
that have grown into one huge metropolis.

253
00:53:47,040 --> 00:53:58,200
Its history is often compared to Shanghai, with its concession era buildings
and commercial energy, but the city also has its own distinct character.

254
00:55:13,600 --> 00:55:21,320
One of the most iconic buildings here is the Yellow Crane Tower,
from where you can see much of the city's skyline,

255
00:55:21,840 --> 00:55:25,880
and also the historic Wuhan Yangte River bridge.

256
00:55:27,560 --> 00:55:37,800
This double-deck road and rail bridge was the first modern bridge to
span the Yangtze river, and was completed in 1957.

257
00:56:12,280 --> 00:56:21,040
We finish this journey through urban China in Guangzhou,
a fascinating city located in the Pearl River Delta.

258
00:56:23,800 --> 00:56:31,080
It's a center of Cantonese culture and has grown into a major
trading and business hub over the years. 

259
00:56:35,400 --> 00:56:43,520
While skyscrapers dominate the skyline, Guangzhou also has a 
variety of neighborhoods with a more relaxed atmosphere.

260
00:56:49,400 --> 00:56:59,680
Downtown Guangzhou is filled with modern office towers, shopping malls,
and a massive football stadium, and is really the commercial heart of the city.

261
00:57:11,600 --> 00:57:17,160
But as you make your way through town, you'll also meet
the other faces of Guangzhou.

262
00:57:41,040 --> 00:57:49,080
The pedestrian only Shamian island, for instance, is filled with
historic mansions and villas.

263
00:57:59,920 --> 00:58:03,720
Also impressive in the Sacred Heart cathedral,

264
00:58:03,720 --> 00:58:09,000
while the nearby riverside promenade is perfect for an evening stroll.

265
00:58:21,800 --> 00:58:30,520
And in the suburbs, I found this traditional South Chinese garden, called Baomo,
filled with temples and classic bridges.

266
00:58:58,800 --> 00:59:07,600
But in the evenings, when the neon lights go on, Guangzhou is
unmistakably a Chinese megacity again.

267
00:59:38,160 --> 00:59:42,160
And that concludes my journey through urban China.

268
00:59:42,720 --> 00:59:50,920
I realize I have only scratched the surface, and you could spend months,
if not years, exploring these places.

269
00:59:53,320 --> 00:59:57,520
Yet I still hope this video gave you some travel ideas,

270
00:59:57,520 --> 01:00:03,520
or at least provided a glimpse into these captivating but complicated cities.

271
01:00:04,760 --> 01:00:09,560
Thanks for watching this video, and I hope to see you again next time.

272
01:00:10,320 --> 01:00:12,280
Travel safely.

